        <body>
  <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in District 79: A Guide for Parents</h2>

  <p>District 79, serving all five boroughs of NYC with alternative schools and programs, presents unique challenges and opportunities regarding student discipline. Understanding your child's rights within this context is paramount. While adhering to the NYC Department of Education's (NYCDOE) citywide standards, District 79 must also tailor disciplinary practices to the specific needs of its diverse student population, many of whom have pre-existing challenges.</p>

  <h3>Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDR): Protecting Students with Disabilities</h3>

  <p>For students with IEPs (Individualized Education Programs) or 504 plans, a <b>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR)</b> is a cornerstone of disciplinary due process. Required *before* a suspension exceeding 10 school days or any expulsion (defined as a change of placement under IDEA), the MDR determines if the student's behavior leading to the disciplinary action was directly caused by, or had a direct and substantial relationship to, the student's disability. It also investigates if the behavior was a direct result of the school's failure to implement the student's IEP or 504 plan. (See 34 CFR § 300.530(e)).</p>

  <p><b>Parent/Guardian Rights in the MDR Process:</b> Your active participation is not just welcomed, it's legally mandated. You have the right to:</p>
  <ul>
        <li><b>Receive Notice:</b> Timely written notification of the MDR meeting, including the purpose, date, time, location, and a list of participants.</li>
        <li><b>Review Records:</b> Access and review all relevant school records pertaining to your child's disability, behavior, and the incident leading to the disciplinary action <i>prior</i> to the MDR meeting.  This includes incident reports, witness statements, disciplinary records, IEP or 504 plan documentation, and any relevant evaluations. (Consider filing a formal request for records under the Freedom of Information Law (FOIL) if necessary to ensure timely access).</li>
        <li><b>Participate Meaningfully:</b>  Attend the MDR meeting, present information, and contribute to the discussion. This includes sharing your perspective on the student’s behavior, the effectiveness of the IEP/504 plan, and any relevant factors that may have contributed to the incident.</li>
        <li><b>Bring an Advocate or Attorney:</b> You have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting to support you and your child.</li>
        <li><b>Receive a Copy of the MDR Determination:</b> You are entitled to a written copy of the MDR determination, including the reasons for the decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>What Happens During an MDR?</b> The IEP team, including the parent/guardian, relevant school personnel (e.g., teachers, administrators, school psychologist), and the student (when appropriate), will review all relevant information, including:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The student's IEP or 504 plan</li>
        <li>Teacher observations</li>
        <li>Relevant information provided by the parents/guardians</li>
        <li>Any relevant evaluation data</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Possible Outcomes of an MDR:</b></p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Manifestation:</b> If the team determines that the behavior *was* a manifestation of the student's disability *or* that the school failed to implement the IEP/504 plan, the student must be returned to their current placement (unless the parents and the NYCDOE agree otherwise). The IEP team must then conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) or revise the existing BIP to address the behavior.  If the behavior was due to a failure to implement the IEP/504 plan, the NYCDOE must take immediate steps to correct the deficiencies.</li>
        <li><b>No Manifestation:</b> If the team determines that the behavior was *not* a manifestation of the student's disability and that the school properly implemented the IEP/504 plan, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities. However, the school must continue to provide educational services to the student during the period of suspension or expulsion to enable the student to continue to participate in the general education curriculum and to progress toward meeting the goals set out in the student's IEP.  (34 CFR § 300.530(d)).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Common Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Pre-Determined Outcomes:</b> Be wary of MDR meetings where the outcome seems pre-determined before the discussion even begins. Ensure your voice is heard and your concerns are addressed.</li>
            <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b> If the IEP/504 plan wasn't being followed, document specific instances and bring this to the MDR team's attention. This is a *critical* factor.</li>
            <li><b>Insufficient Information:</b> Make sure all relevant information is presented to the team, including outside evaluations, medical records, and your observations of your child's behavior.</li>
            <li><b>Procedural Violations:</b> Any violation of the procedural safeguards outlined in IDEA can be grounds for challenging the MDR determination.</li>
        </ul>
    
  <h3>Suspensions in District 79: Know Your Rights and Responsibilities</h3>

  <p>If your child faces a suspension in District 79, understanding your rights and responsibilities is critical. You have the right to be informed of the charges against your child in detail and to present their side of the story.  Refer to the NYCDOE's <a href="https://www.schools.nyc.gov/school-life/school-discipline">Discipline Code</a> for specific offenses and potential consequences.</p>

    <p><b>Key Steps to Take When Your Child is Suspended:</b></p>

    <ol>
        <li><b>Immediate Action:</b> Contact the school immediately to understand the reason for the suspension and the length of the suspension. Request a written explanation of the charges.</li>
        <li><b>Documentation:</b> Request all documentation related to the incident, including incident reports, witness statements, and any video or audio recordings.</li>
        <li><b>Meeting Attendance:</b> Attend all scheduled meetings with school administrators. Bring an advocate or attorney if you feel it's necessary.</li>
        <li><b>Student's Perspective:</b> Help your child prepare to explain their perspective on the incident.</li>
        <li><b>Appeal Process:</b> Understand the appeal process and deadlines. In NYC, suspensions exceeding five days can be appealed to the Superintendent. The appeal must be in writing and state the reasons for the appeal.</li>
        <li><b>MDR (If Applicable):</b> If your child has an IEP or 504 plan, ensure an MDR is conducted promptly and correctly *before* the suspension exceeds 10 cumulative school days in a school year or involves a change of placement.</li>
        <li><b>Educational Services:</b> Ensure your child receives appropriate educational services during the suspension period to continue their academic progress.  For suspensions exceeding 10 days, the district must provide services to the extent required under IDEA and Part 200 of the NYS Regulations.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><b>Advocacy Tips for Suspensions:</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communications with the school, including dates, times, and the names of people you spoke with.</li>
            <li><b>Request Written Policies:</b> Obtain a copy of the school's disciplinary policies and procedures.</li>
            <li><b>Focus on Facts:</b> When presenting your child's side of the story, focus on the facts and avoid emotional arguments.</li>
            <li><b>Highlight Mitigating Circumstances:</b> Point out any mitigating circumstances that may have contributed to the incident, such as provocation, peer influence, or underlying emotional issues.</li>
            <li><b>Emphasize Cooperation:</b> Show that you are willing to work with the school to address the underlying issues and prevent future incidents.</li>
        </ul>

    <h3>Due Process Rights: Protecting Your Child's Educational Future</h3>

    <p>The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law provide parents of children with disabilities with significant due process rights to challenge decisions made by the school district regarding their child's education.  These rights are especially important in disciplinary situations where a student faces suspension, expulsion, or a change of placement.</p>

    <p><b>Key Due Process Rights:</b></p>

    <ul>
        <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> The right to an impartial due process hearing to resolve disputes with the school district regarding the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child, or the provision of a free appropriate public education (FAPE). (See 34 CFR § 300.507).</li>
        <li><b>Right to Counsel:</b> The right to be represented by an attorney or advocate at the due process hearing.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Present Evidence:</b> The right to present evidence, including documents and witnesses, to support your case.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Cross-Examine Witnesses:</b> The right to cross-examine witnesses presented by the school district.</li>
        <li><b>Right to a Written Decision:</b> The right to a written decision from the impartial hearing officer that addresses the issues raised in the complaint.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Appeal:</b> The right to appeal the decision of the impartial hearing officer to a state review officer (SRO).</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Filing a Due Process Complaint:</b></p>

    <p>A due process complaint must be filed within two years of the date you knew or should have known about the action that forms the basis of the complaint (See 34 CFR § 300.507). The complaint must contain specific information, including:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>The name and address of the child</li>
        <li>The name of the school the child attends</li>
        <li>A description of the problem, including the facts and circumstances related to the problem</li>
        <li>A proposed resolution of the problem, to the extent known and available to the parent at the time</li>
    </ul>

    <p><b>Resolution Session:</b></p>

    <p>Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution session with the parents and relevant members of the IEP team. The purpose of the resolution session is to provide an opportunity for the parties to resolve the dispute without going to a hearing. If the dispute is not resolved within 30 days, the due process hearing can proceed.</p>

    <p><b>When Due Process is Relevant in Disciplinary Cases:</b></p>

    <ul>
        <li><b>Challenging an MDR Determination:</b> If you disagree with the outcome of the MDR, you can file a due process complaint.</li>
        <li><b>Challenging a Suspension or Expulsion:</b> If you believe that a suspension or expulsion is discriminatory or violates your child's rights, you can file a due process complaint.</li>
        <li><b>Failure to Provide FAPE During Suspension:</b> If the school district fails to provide appropriate educational services to your child during a period of suspension, you can file a due process complaint.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: My child was suspended for fighting. Does the MDR still apply?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: Yes, if your child has an IEP or 504 plan, an MDR is required before a suspension exceeding 10 cumulative school days or any expulsion, regardless of the reason for the suspension. The MDR must determine if the behavior was a manifestation of your child's disability or due to a failure to implement the IEP/504 plan.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What if my child doesn't have an official IEP but I suspect they have a disability?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: If you suspect your child has a disability, you should request a formal evaluation from the NYCDOE. While an MDR might not be immediately required, proactively seeking an evaluation is crucial. The school must respond to your request within a reasonable timeframe, and failure to do so could be grounds for a complaint.  Consider requesting the evaluation in writing. The Committee on Special Education (CSE) will then conduct the evaluation to determine if your child is eligible for special education services. In some cases, you may be able to argue for "stay put" rights (maintaining the child's current placement) pending the outcome of the evaluation, particularly if the school was aware of potential disabilities. (34 CFR § 300.518). Note that even without an IEP or 504 plan, students still have general due process rights related to discipline.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be expelled from District 79?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: Expulsion, defined as a change of placement under IDEA, is a serious disciplinary action. Students with IEPs or 504 plans have additional protections, including the MDR process. Expulsion is generally reserved for severe or repeated offenses. The NYCDOE must provide due process protections before expelling any student, regardless of disability status.  Expulsion proceedings will involve a Superintendent's suspension hearing.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What are "stay put" rights in the context of discipline?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: "Stay put" rights, as outlined in IDEA (34 CFR § 300.518), generally mean that during any dispute regarding a child's IEP or placement, the child remains in their current educational placement *unless* the parents and the NYCDOE agree otherwise.  In disciplinary situations, there are exceptions. School personnel can remove a student to an interim alternative educational setting (IAES) for up to 45 school days for certain offenses, such as possessing a weapon or drugs at school, or inflicting serious bodily injury on another person.  However, even in an IAES, the student must continue to receive educational services that enable them to participate in the general education curriculum and to progress toward meeting the goals set out in their IEP.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What is an Interim Alternative Educational Setting (IAES)?</b></dt>
        <dd>A: An IAES is a temporary placement for students who have been suspended or removed from their current placement for disciplinary reasons. The IAES must be designed to enable the student to continue to participate in the general education curriculum and to progress toward meeting the goals set out in their IEP. The IEP team determines the services to be provided in the IAES. Placement in an IAES for more than 45 school days requires either parental agreement or a decision by an impartial hearing officer.</dd>
    </dl>
</body>
